\section{Blocks}{

\code{blocks} is either Stata's block specification as one string --
\code{"sheet(Block A) rows(2/5) cols(B-D) label(Any use) \\\\ sheet(Block B) ..."}
(blocks separated by a backslash; sub-options \code{sheet()}, \code{rows()},
\code{cols()}, \code{label()}, \code{skip()}, and \code{postfix()}) -- or a list whose
elements are each a \code{tt_table} or a list with the same fields
(\code{sheet} or \code{table}, \code{rows}, \code{cols}, \code{label}, \code{skip}, \code{postfix}).
\itemize{
\item \code{sheet}: a sheet of the workbook \code{xlsx}. Its values are read as text;
formatting is not copied (Stata re-applies stacktab's own style too).
Without \code{rows} and \code{cols} the block is the sheet's whole used range
(from its first to its last used row and column, so a table written
from \code{B2} with a title in \code{A1} includes the title row and the gutter
column). \code{rows} and \code{cols} together select that cell range in sheet
coordinates, which must lie inside the used range. \code{cols} alone keeps
those sheet columns of the used range, and \code{rows} alone those sheet
rows of it (as in tabtools 2.1.12; 2.1.11 counted \code{rows} alone from
the first used row).
\item \code{table} (R only): a \code{tt_table}. The block is its header rows and body as
its CSV sink shows them (no title or footnote); \code{rows} and \code{cols} count
within that grid (\code{cols = "A"} is the label column).
\item \code{rows}: \code{"lo/hi"} (or \code{"lo hi"}, as Stata also accepts), one row, or a
range such as \code{2:5}. \code{cols}: Excel letters, \code{"B-D"} (or \code{"B D"}),
\code{"B"}, or \code{c("B", "D")}.
\item \code{skip}: drop the n-th row of the selected block, a positive whole
number (a number beyond the block's last row, such as 9 for a
two-row block, drops nothing; 0 and negative numbers are errors, as in
Stata). \code{label}: replace the
first cell of the block's first row. \code{postfix}: append a space and this
text to every cell of the block's first column.
}

Under \code{layout = "vstack"} the blocks' rows follow one another (a block
narrower than the widest is padded with blank cells), with \code{spacing}
blank rows between blocks; the first row of every block is a section row,
bold between thin rules. Under \code{"hstack"} the blocks must have the same
number of rows and are placed side by side.
}

\section{Column merges}{

\code{columnmerge} takes Stata's pieces, \code{"B+C as aHR (95\% CI)"}, several in a
character vector or separated by a backslash. The two columns are named
by their letter in the composite as assembled (\code{A} is its first column)
or as \verb{_xcol2}; letters keep their meaning across pieces even after an
earlier merge removed a column. Each cell of the second column, when not
blank or \code{"."}, is appended to the first after a space, the second
column is dropped, and the header text replaces the merged cell of every
section row (the first row under \code{hstack}).
}

\section{Differences from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item \code{tt_table} blocks and a missing \code{xlsx} are R additions.
\item Numeric and date cells of a source sheet are converted to text by
openxlsx2 (\code{0.33333333333333331}, \code{2020-01-06}) and generally differ
from Stata's \code{allstring} text (\code{.3333333333333333}, \code{  1/6/2020}); the
tabtools commands write text cells only, so their sheets read the same
on both sides.
\item Block sheet names are matched with case, as Stata's \verb{import excel}
does; the output \code{sheet} is matched without regard to case, as
Stata's \code{stacktab} does.
\item Side-by-side (\code{hstack}) \code{tt_table} blocks whose rows differ at a
position (by the table's row keys, else the row labels) give a warning
naming them; the rows are still placed by position, as in Stata.
\item In block mode the files are written together at the end, as in Stata;
a failure while copying them into place restores replaced files.
In \code{frames} mode, as in native Stata, \code{\link[=puttab]{puttab()}} writes CSV, Markdown
and Excel sequentially after validating source metadata and targets;
a later write failure can leave an earlier sink written. Existing
output sheets are replaced while unrelated sheets are preserved.
There is no \code{frame()}: the returned table is the composite.
\item Stata's \verb{stacktab: N blocks -> N rows written -> sheet S} line, printed
when a workbook is written, is a message; nothing is printed for the
CSV and Markdown targets.
}
}

\examples{
primary <- data.frame(term = c("Any HRT", "Former smoker"),
                      ahr = c("0.82", "1.14"),
                      ci = c("(0.69, 0.98)", "(0.97, 1.34)"))
dose <- data.frame(term = c("Low dose", "High dose"), ahr = c("0.91", "0.73"),
                   ci = c("(0.74, 1.12)", "(0.58, 0.92)"))
for (d in c("primary", "dose")) {
  x <- get(d)
  attr(x$term, "label") <- "Exposure"
  attr(x$ahr, "label") <- "aHR"
  attr(x$ci, "label") <- "95\% CI"
  assign(d, x)
}
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx")
puttab(primary, xlsx = path, sheet = "Primary", varlabels = TRUE, title = "Primary")
puttab(dose, xlsx = path, sheet = "Dose", varlabels = TRUE, title = "Dose")
stacktab("sheet(Primary) rows(2/4) cols(B-D) label(Any HRT use) \\\\
          sheet(Dose) rows(2/4) cols(B-D) label(By estrogen dose)",
         xlsx = path, sheet = "Composite",
         columnmerge = "B+C as aHR (95\% CI)",
         title = "Hormone therapy and recurrent events", display = TRUE)

# The same composite from the tables themselves (R only)
a <- puttab(primary, varlabels = TRUE)
b <- puttab(dose, varlabels = TRUE)
stacktab(list(list(table = a, label = "Any HRT use"),
              list(table = b, label = "By estrogen dose")),
         columnmerge = "B+C as aHR (95\% CI)")

# Frames are panels with one common header
stacktab(frames = list(primary = list(data = primary, label = "Primary"),
                       dose = list(data = dose, label = "Dose")))
}
